  "image_name": "1bf3bbef3244202977666ee4d075aa0a.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a solid tumor microenvironment showing how “nanotheranostic” particles move through and act within tumor tissue. A dense mass of blue cancer cells sits beside paler healthy cells, threaded by abnormal, tortuous blood vessels and extracellular matrix. The left and right margins indicate gradients from normoxia to hypoxia and from low to high interstitial fluid pressure, emphasizing that conditions become harsher deeper in the tumor.\n\nSmall red particles represent nanotheranostic agents. The image highlights three main stages: they leak out of defective tumor blood vessels, spread through the tumor, and then interact locally with cells to enable imaging and drug release. Insets at the bottom summarize these steps: extravasation from a leaky vessel, intratumoral release of imaging and therapeutic components, and interactions with stromal cells such as tumor-associated macrophages and cancer-associated fibroblasts, which can take up or expel the particles.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that nanoparticle delivery in tumors depends not just on the blood vessel leakiness, but also on the complex tumor microenvironment, including hypoxia, pressure, stromal cells, and the extracellular matrix, which together influence where particles accumulate and how effectively they work.",
